    ($SATQ$699af7b3$SATQ$, $SATQ$36bef1c8-d853-462f-a9e8-7575812e84e9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Percentages$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The weight of sample A is <math alttext="468 percent sign"><mn>468</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the weight of sample B, and the weight of sample A is <math alttext="0.780 percent sign"><mn>0.780</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the weight of sample C. If the weight of sample C is <math alttext="p percent sign"><mi>p</mi><mo>%</mo></math> of the weight of sample B, what is the value of <math alttext="p"><mi>p</mi>
</math>?</p>$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$["60000"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["60000"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>The correct answer is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60,000"><mn>60,000</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that the weight of sample A is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="468 percent sign"><mn>468</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the weight of sample B&nbsp;and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="0.780 percent sign"><mn>0.780</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the weight of sample C. Let the weight of sample B be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="b"><mi>b</mi></math> and the weight of sample C be <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c"><mi>c</mi></math>. Then, the weight of sample A is equivalent to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 468 b Over 100 EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>468</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4.68 b"><mn>4.68</mn><mi>b</mi></math>, and also equivalent to <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 0 period 780 c Over 100 EndFraction"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>780</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="0.00780 c"><mn>0.00780</mn><mi>c</mi></math>. Setting these expressions equal to each other yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="4.68 b equals 0.00780 c"><mn>4.68</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.00780</mn><mi>c</mi></math>. Dividing both sides of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="0.00780"><mn>0.00780</mn></math> yields&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 4.68 b Over 0.00780 EndFraction equals c"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4.68</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow><mn>0.00780</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c equals 600 b"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mi>b</mi></math>. If the weight of sample C is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="p percent sign"><mi>p</mi><mo>%</mo></math> of the weight of sample B, then <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction b"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mi>b</mi></math>. Since <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c equals 600 b"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>600</mn><mi>b</mi></math> and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="c equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction b"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mi>b</mi></math>, it follows that <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="600 b equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction b"><mn>600</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mi>b</mi></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="600 equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction"><mn>600</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="100"><mn>100</mn></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60,000 equals p"><mn>60,000</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></math>. Therefore, the value of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="p"><mi>p</mi></math> is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="60,000"><mn>60,000</mn></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"699af7b3","external_id":"36bef1c8-d853-462f-a9e8-7575812e84e9","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"726d5b93-0414-4cc1-b1f1-8fafc4013950","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd"