><mi>b</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"c equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction b\"><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mi>b</mi></math>, it follows that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"600 b equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction b\"><mn>600</mn><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mi>b</mi></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"600 equals StartFraction p Over 100 EndFraction\"><mn>600</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>p</mi><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"100\"><mn>100</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60,000 equals p\"><mn>60,000</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></math>. Therefore, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"p\"><mi>p</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"60,000\"><mn>60,000</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"36bef1c8-d853-462f-a9e8-7575812e84e9","correct_answer":["60000"]},"createDate":1776362727992,"updateDate":1776362727992}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727992, 1776362727992),
    ($SATQ$69f6717f$SATQ$, $SATQ$cd36e81a-3f06-40da-ab13-b7cd41cd0e9b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$12449f40-c91e-432d-a9af-9d724af90a75$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A sample of oak has a density of <math alttext="807"><mn>807</mn>
</math><cite>&nbsp;</cite>kilograms per cubic meter. The sample is in the shape of a cube, where each edge has a length of <math alttext="0.90"><mn>0.90</mn></math><cite>&nbsp;</cite>meters. To the nearest whole number, what is the mass, in kilograms, of this sample?&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"ab88e744-cfef-419b-b5c9-ffb0d03cb81f","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"588\"><mn>588</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"6d69b27b-8c03-4da3-bb2e-8798696d23ee","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"726\"><mn>726</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dced1692-d06d-4e6f-859c-d76681d329e0","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"897\"><mn>897</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d0431fd5-920c-4648-8b14-a2c9334e2e71","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"1,107\"><mn>1,107</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ab88e744-cfef-419b-b5c9-ffb0d03cb81f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the sample is in the shape of a cube with edge lengths of <math alttext="0.9"><mn>0.9</mn>
</math> meters. Therefore, the volume of the sample is <math alttext="0.90 cubed"><msup><mn>0.90</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="0.729"><mn>0.729</mn>
</math>, cubic meters. It&rsquo;s also given that the sample has a density of <math alttext="807"><mn>807</mn>
</math> kilograms per <math alttext="1"><mn>1</mn>
</math> cubic meter. Therefore, the mass of this sample is <math alttext="0.729 cubic meters left parenthesis StartFraction 807 kilograms Over 1 cubic meter EndFraction right parenthesis"><mn>0.729</mn><mtext>&nbsp;cubic&nbsp;meters</mtext><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>807</mn><mtext>&nbsp;kilograms</mtext></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext>&nbsp;cubic&nbsp;meter</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="588.303"><mn>588.303</mn>
</math> kilograms. Rounding this mass to the nearest whole number gives <math alttext="588"><mn>588</mn>
</math> kilograms. Therefore, to the nearest whole number, the mass, in kilograms, of this sample is <math alttext="588"><mn>588</mn>
</math>.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C&nbsp;is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice D&nbsp;is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"69f6717f","external_id":"cd36e81a-3f06-40da-ab13-b7cd41cd0e9b","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"12449f40-c91e-432d-a9af-9d724af90a75","uId":"35add8a9-5ab0-4896-8836-e553f5ea3d9d","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A sample of oak has a density of <math alttext=\"807\"><mn>807</mn>\n</math><cite>&nbsp;</cite>kilograms per cubic meter. The sample is in the shape of a cube, where each edge has a length of <math alttext=\"0.90\"><mn>0.90</mn></math><cite>&nbsp;</cite>meters. To the nearest whole number, what is the mass, in kilograms, of this sample?&nbsp;</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"ab88e744-cfef-419b-b5c9-ffb0d03cb81f","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"588\"><mn>588</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"6d69b27b-8c03-4da3-bb2e-8798696d23ee","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"726\"><mn>726</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"dced1692-d06d-4e6f-859c-d76681d329e0","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"897\"><mn>897</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d0431fd5-920c-4648-8b14-a2c9334e2e71","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><math alttext=\"1,107\"><mn>1,107</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["ab88e744-cfef-419b-b5c9-ffb0d03cb81f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. It&rsquo;s given that the sample is in the shape of a cube with edge lengths of <math alttext=\"0.9\"><mn>0.9</mn>\n</math> meters. Therefore, the volume of the sample is <math alttext=\"0.90 cubed\"><msup><mn>0.90</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext=\"0.